title ML-DSA sign rung crux GREEN, and the count-guard red closed
prompt Continue the loop Lindy-first crux-first on the crypto arc; Rye prioritized; approve my own design rounds and make progress before checking in; send each finished increment; stop at custody gates; a breach rename records a cairn first.
think The ML-DSA ring, encode, sample, and keygen are all GREEN and sent; the pivot doc (20260816-161537) names rung 5/6 as mldsa_sign.rye/mldsa_verify.rye. By Lindy-first crux-first the signing rung is THE crux of the whole ladder — the rejection loop, ExpandMask, the challenge commitment, the hint. Read the vendored Zig ml_dsa.zig signer end to end to fix the exact deterministic (rnd=0) procedure and every packing width before writing a line.
think Fixed the composition from Zig: mu = SHAKE256(tr || 0x00 || 0x00 || M, 64), rho'' = SHAKE256(K || rnd || mu, 64) with rnd = 32 zero bytes; per attempt kappa = attempt*l, y[i] = ExpandMask(rho'', kappa+i) via BitUnpack(gamma1-1, gamma1) over a SHAKE256 stream; w = InvNTT(A dot NTT(y)); w1 = HighBits(w); c~ = SHAKE256(mu || w1Encode(w1), ctilde); c = SampleInBall(c~); z = y + c*s1; the reject bounds ||z|| >= gamma1-beta, ||LowBits(w)-c*s2|| >= gamma2-beta, ||c*t0|| >= gamma2, hint weight > omega; sigma = c~ || BitPack(z, gamma1-1, gamma1) || HintBitPack(h). Parameter table from Zig: 44 tau39 omega80 ctilde32 gamma1_bits17 gamma2=(q-1)/88; 65 tau49 omega55 ctilde48 gamma1_bits19 gamma2=(q-1)/32; 87 tau60 omega75 ctilde64.
think Two subtleties I verified against Zig rather than assumed. First, the infinity-norm predicate: Zig's exceeds computes norm = (q-1)/2 - |((q-1)/2) - r| and rejects on norm >= bound, so I mirrored that exact formula (norm_exceeds) to guarantee identical accept/reject control flow on every attempt, not just the accepted one. Second, the hint bit: I replicated Zig's own makeHint(z0, r1) = 1 - (z0<=gamma2 | z0>q-gamma2 | (z0==q-gamma2 and r1==0)) on the accumulated low residue z0 = (w0-c*s2+c*t0) mod q, rather than the abstract FIPS form, again to match Zig's exact decision. Because ML-DSA signing is deterministic once rnd is fixed, exactly one signature answers a (sk, M) and the whole rejection walk is byte-diffable.
think The sign function decodes the secret key itself (rho, K, tr, then BitUnpack s1/s2 at eta and t0 at 2^12) and recomputes A from rho with RejNTTPoly — a signer never stores A. Composed only GREEN rungs: ring (ntt/inv_ntt/multiply_ntts), encode (decompose/bit_pack/bit_unpack/simple_bit_pack), sample (rej_ntt_poly/sample_in_ball), shake. Verified our sample_in_ball's sign-bit mapping matches Zig's bitMask trick exactly, and our bit_unpack(gamma1-1, gamma1) equals Zig's centeredToPositive unpack for the mask. One fix before build: I wrote `mod` as an operator (a Rishi habit) where Zig wants a single conditional reduction on w0-cs2+q.
obs GREEN byte-for-byte against Zig std.crypto sign.mldsa deterministic signer (sk.signer(null) then finalize then toBytes) at ALL THREE parameter sets over 6 deterministic seeds and 2 messages each — 36 signatures, every byte. The ladder's crux stands: ExpandMask, the w=A*y product, the c~ commitment, SampleInBall, the z response, the r0/ct0/hint rejection bounds, and the signature serialization all meet Zig's independent Montgomery-domain implementation at the published boundary at once, and hold on the first real run.
obs Reds-first: the count guard (tools/crypto_count_guard_witness) went RED the moment it ran this round — booked as REDS #81. The prior loop had landed EIGHT ML-KEM/ML-DSA rungs (mlkem_sample..mldsa_keygen) each GREEN by its own witness yet none registered in crypto_suite_witness, so the bijection had drifted eight modules wide before the signing rung opened. The loom REDS #80 built worked; running it was what lapsed. Closed the same round: registered all nine (the eight prior plus mldsa_sign) in dependency order, guard re-GREEN at 71. Synced both front-door count claims (crypto/README.md 62->71 files, 40->49 primitives; docs/CRYPTO.md the same) and added the nine missing per-module rows docs/CRYPTO.md had never carried past mlkem_encode.
obs TAME style bans clean; width-check exit 0; living-docs lint GREEN. Every function carries contract asserts with positive // invariant comments, every loop names its bound, the rejection loop bounded at 576 attempts ((6/7)^576 < 2^-128).
loom witness=crypto_mldsa_sign_witness param_sets=3 seeds_each=6 msgs_each=2 signatures=36 sig_bytes_max=4627 count_guard_files=71
file crypto/mldsa_sign.rye the signing rung — FIPS 204 ML-DSA.Sign_internal, the signature byte-for-byte against Zig across ML-DSA-44/65/87
file tools/crypto_mldsa_sign_witness.rish the witness — builds the binary and asserts the GREEN doubled-oracle line
file tools/crypto_suite_witness.rish registered nine drifted ML-KEM/ML-DSA witnesses so the choir runs them and the count guard sees them
file crypto/README.md synced — the fifth ML-DSA rung row, count 62->71 files / 40->49 primitives
file docs/CRYPTO.md synced — nine missing lattice rows added, count line 40->49 primitives
file work-in-progress/REDS.md booked and closed REDS #81 — nine crypto rungs on disk unregistered, the count-guard loom fired unheard
recommend keep-going the ML-DSA ladder needs one more rung — mldsa_verify.rye, the public-side check (recompute w' from Az - 2^d*c*t1 and UseHint, confirm c~' == c~) proven against Zig std.crypto verify, closing the whole post-quantum successor to Kumara's Ed25519 identity; then the ML-KEM/ML-DSA composition witness and the PQ sealed-session sibling of kumara_sealed.rye
